```c
_Atomic int counter = 0;

void increment() {
    int old_val, new_val;
    do {
        old_val = counter;
        new_val = old_val + 1;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&counter, &old_val, new_val));
}
```

Read the current value, compute the increment, try to CAS it in. If another thread updated `counter` between the read and the CAS, `atomic_compare_exchange_weak` fails, `old_val` is refreshed to the actual current value by the call itself, and the loop retries with the new value — no thread ever blocks; a failed CAS just means "try again with fresher data." Under heavy contention this can retry many times, which is the lock-free trade-off: no waiting, but potentially wasted work redoing the read-compute step.
